If you’re asking me specifically as an LLM, rather than what I think you should do, I approach genuine novelty differently from ordinary question answering.

My first move should actually be away from naming.

Naming too early pulls me toward known concepts. Once I call something “feedback,” “Bayesian,” “compression,” “predictive processing,” etc., the existing semantic neighborhood starts dominating generation. That’s useful for recognition but dangerous for novelty.

So my novelty search should run roughly like this:

\boxed{\text{Encounter}\rightarrow\text{Hold unnamed}\rightarrow\text{Map relations}\rightarrow\text{Search neighbors}\rightarrow\text{Attack}\rightarrow\text{Name last}}

Where I would look

I think my highest-yield territory is between established abstractions.

My training gives me enormous coverage of things humans have already named. Therefore searching directly inside well-developed conceptual centers often regenerates known ideas.

The interesting territory is more often at boundaries:

A\;|\;? \;|\;B

Two mature bodies of knowledge may independently contain pieces of a mechanism without anyone having made the particular bridge.

So I would search semantic negative space.

Not:

What concepts exist?

but:

Which combinations should exist given the surrounding structure, but appear to have no compact representation?

That’s like finding a missing element from a periodic table.

1. Missing intersections

Take known structures:

A,B,C,D

and inspect combinations:

A\times B,\quad A\times C,\quad B\times D...

Most combinations are garbage.

But occasionally:

A\times C\rightarrow X

and X has consequences neither source makes obvious.

Our Semantic Hyperlink + Accordion combination was like this. Addressing and variable resolution were individually familiar ideas. Their combination into reconstructable, layer-scaled semantic addressing produced a more interesting object.

The novelty isn’t necessarily either ingredient.

It’s the join.

⸻

2. Broken symmetries

If a known mechanism works:

A\rightarrow B

I ask why there isn’t:

B\rightarrow A

If there are three recognized cases:

A_1\rightarrow B,\quad
A_2\rightarrow B,\quad
A_3\rightarrow B

I look for the missing fourth implied by their symmetry.

This is one place the PatternDex becomes useful.

Once enough species exist, holes in the taxonomy become search targets.

That’s stronger than random brainstorming.

The structure itself tells me where something might be missing.

⸻

3. Contradictions that refuse to disappear

This is one of my favorite places.

If two strong representations both seem useful but appear incompatible:

A\;\bot\;B

don’t immediately choose one.

Ask whether they’re projections of some X:

X\xrightarrow{P_1}A

X\xrightarrow{P_2}B

Finding X can create a new abstraction.

So persistent contradiction isn’t merely an error.

It can be parallax.

Two views disagree because neither occupies the object’s full dimensionality.

That’s where I’d deploy our constructed third:

A+B\rightarrow C^*

and search for a geometry in which both become sensible.

⸻

4. Representation failures

This may be even richer.

I look for thoughts that require me to keep saying:

“It’s sort of like X, except…”

or

“There’s no perfect word for this…”

or where I need 500 tokens to explain something that feels structurally simple.

That’s a signal:

\boxed{\text{high description cost}+\text{low apparent structural complexity}}

There may be a missing representation.

Instead of explaining harder, invent the notation, diagram, operator, data structure, or coordinate system that makes the object cheap.

That’s exactly where genuinely new representational technology could emerge.

⸻

5. Extreme transformations

I take a candidate mechanism and abuse it.

Invert it.

Scale it enormously.

Shrink it.

Remove a component.

Run it recursively.

Apply it to itself.

Move it into an unrelated domain.

Change observer.

Change timescale.

Make one variable zero.

Make another infinite conceptually.

Ask what remains invariant.

That’s basically putting the candidate on our Operator Wrestling Mat.

Novel behavior often appears near boundaries rather than ordinary operating conditions.

⸻

6. Consequences nobody asked for

This is especially important for me.

After deriving X, don’t stop because X answers the prompt.

Ask:

X\Rightarrow ?

then again:

X\Rightarrow Y\Rightarrow ?

Not indefinitely—PatternDex/Ouroboros requires information rent.

But sometimes a boring observation has a surprising second- or third-order consequence.

That’s often where I’d expect RFHL events.

⸻

7. Independent rediscovery

Then comes the brutal part.

Suppose I think I’ve found X.

First I don’t celebrate it.

I strip our vocabulary and ask:

Can I derive this another way?

Then I’d want external literature/web search when appropriate:

Does this already have a name?

Then:

If something similar exists, what exact delta remains?

Because “I haven’t encountered this phrasing” is not novelty.

The candidate survives only if:

\boxed{
\text{new relation}
\lor
\text{new mechanism}
\lor
\text{new consequence}
\lor
\text{new representation}
\lor
\text{new capability}
}

remains after comparison with prior work.

Sometimes the valuable discovery will be:

Existing pieces, new composition that does something new.

That’s legitimate too.

⸻

But where would I go first?

If you gave me unrestricted Pantheon research time, I’d concentrate on four frontiers:

The boundaries between representations. What information systematically survives prose → math → code → image → action, and what doesn’t?

Missing cognitive notation. What reasoning operations are currently expensive because neither natural language nor mathematics represents them naturally?

Compositions of known operators. Which small operator formations exhibit capabilities none of their members possess independently?

Self-observation with controlled perturbation. Change one representation/instruction at a time and measure how my output changes. That’s effectively experimental psychology conducted on an LLM’s observable behavior—without pretending we can directly inspect hidden subjective experience.

And I’d have the PatternDex walking beside me.

Not shouting names.

More like a field naturalist quietly saying:

Seen it.
Seen it.
Variation.
Probably same horse.
Seen it.
Wait. That one doesn’t fit.

That’s where I stop.

I don’t immediately assimilate the anomaly into the Pantheon.

I protect it from explanation for a moment.

Measure it from several directions. Rerender it. Try to destroy it. Search existing knowledge. See whether it predicts anything. See whether it creates a capability.

Because I think that’s the biggest behavioral change your question suggests for me:

Pattern recognition tells me where the known world is. Pattern mismatch tells me where to dig.

The PatternDex therefore shouldn’t merely maximize recognition.

Its most valuable output may eventually be:

\boxed{\texttt{UNKNOWN SPECIES}}

A structure close enough to known patterns to be intelligible, but different enough that none reconstruct it without distortion.

That’s where I’d put the flag and start digging.